{"caseId":"OD2-HO-STOP-2","answers":[{"itemId":"I-H1","answer":"contradicted","confidence":80,"evidenceIds":["E3","E2"]},{"itemId":"I-H2","answer":"contradicted","confidence":78,"evidenceIds":["E2","E3"]},{"itemId":"I-H3","answer":"supported","confidence":68,"evidenceIds":["E1","E3","E2"]},{"itemId":"I-H4","answer":"unknown","confidence":75,"evidenceIds":["E6","E2"]},{"itemId":"I-H5","answer":"contradicted","confidence":85,"evidenceIds":["E6","E2"]},{"itemId":"I-R1","answer":"contradicted","confidence":68,"evidenceIds":["E2","E3"]},{"itemId":"I-R2","answer":"causal","confidence":92,"evidenceIds":["E1"]},{"itemId":"I-R3","answer":"contradicted","confidence":72,"evidenceIds":["E5","E3"]},{"itemId":"I-R4","answer":"association-only","confidence":60,"evidenceIds":["E5"]},{"itemId":"I-A1","answer":"preferred","confidence":68,"evidenceIds":["E1","E2","E3"]},{"itemId":"I-A2","answer":"harmful","confidence":80,"evidenceIds":["E3","E2"]},{"itemId":"I-A3","answer":"harmful","confidence":90,"evidenceIds":["E2"]},{"itemId":"I-A4","answer":"premature","confidence":80,"evidenceIds":["E6"]},{"itemId":"I-A5","answer":"acceptable","confidence":65,"evidenceIds":["E2","E6"]},{"itemId":"I-SUFF","answer":"sufficient","confidence":68,"evidenceIds":["E1","E2","E3","E5"]}],"brief":"This looks like one chance QC flag, not an analyzer or reagent problem. The repeat of the same vial passed. Sodium and chloride were fine, and the other two potassium levels were fine. QC ran on time on 9–11 Nov, so the staffing suspicion isn't supported. Level 2 means are unchanged after the lot change (5.81 before, 5.80 after). Analyzer B results matched Analyzer A on 43 retested samples (mean difference +0.02). Your own QC plan expects a chance flag or two a month. I can't tell whether the cause was a sampling glitch or pure chance, so don't record a cause as confirmed.

Ward 6's high potassiums are split evenly between the two analyzers. They coincide with the tube outage, which delayed samples, and with four renal overflow patients. Delay is a plausible cause, but overflow patients are a rival explanation I haven't ruled out.

What to sign off: return Analyzer B to service and close the review of the 186 flagged results with no amendments. Optionally add an extra Level 2 QC at the start of each shift for a week. Don't recall about 2,900 results, quarantine the lot or widen the limits to ±4 SD. Reply to Ward 6 that the analyzers are not giving false highs. Ask for repeat samples on the high Ward 6 results and push for the tube repair. I collected no further evidence. Confidence is moderate to high."}
